Original commit body from fc992a2b820af76d47f4a7dac00ce135bf9b55a9:

The BFS barrier dropped the subtree beyond a sanitizer, so a flow a developer
had deliberately protected produced EXACTLY the output of a flow that does not
exist: nothing. A reader could not tell "no path from this source to this sink"
from "a path exists and your encrypt() call is what makes it safe" -- and the
second is what they need before deleting that call.

TaintFlowFinding.sanitized existed for this and was written False at both and
only construction sites, so verify_claims' `and not f.sanitized` was a
TAUTOLOGY and the confirmed_safe branch of the verdict property was unreachable
in production. Owner ruling 2026-08-03: emit the flow labelled.

UNSANITIZED WINS. A sink the taint reaches by any unprotected route is reported
unsanitized even when another route encrypts; labelling it safe because SOME
path sanitizes is the dangerous direction of wrong. Where two distinct sink
SITES differ (one protected, one not) both are now reported and distinguished
by the label -- previously only the violation survived, leaving a reader unable
to tell "the other route is safe" from "there is no other route".

ONE BARRIER IMPLEMENTATION, TWO CALLERS. The structural and DDG propagators
each carried a copy of the walk and had ALREADY DRIFTED -- the structural one
tracked sanitized_nodes, the DDG one dropped them on the floor. That is the
shape that produces "fixed in Python, still broken in Go", so both now share
_reachability_past_sanitizers.

The count is disclosed, not just the label. ClaimVerdict.sanitized_flows
reports how many flows matched the claim but were not counted because a
sanitizer lies on every route, and the CONFIRMED verdict says so in prose --
that is where it matters most, because the claim reads clean and the reason it
reads clean may be one function call away from deletion. Sibling of
excluded_flows, same D7 shape. Envelope schema 1.5 -> 1.6.

A TEST THAT PINNED THE SILENT DROP WAS VACUOUS (L17). test_sanitized_path_no_
violation asserted len(findings) == 0 using a bare py:external:0-0:write_text
sink -- but the sink entry is kind="method" with module pathlib.Path, and a
bare unresolved method call carrying no module context is suppressed outright
(io-boundary:F3 / INV-tapat). Measured on the pre-change code: 0 findings WITH
the sanitizer and 0 WITHOUT it, so the barrier the test names was never
exercised. Fixture corrected to the module-qualified form its own sibling
already used, plus a non-vacuity floor.

MEASURED, 9-repo cohort: zero verdicts move, and ZERO sanitized flows are
produced. The null was pre-registered and its cause is structural rather than
incidental -- the taint labels the cohort's claims constrain (host_secret,
untrusted_input) and the labels any shipped sanitizer can transform
(plaintext, key_material) are DISJOINT SETS. No sanitizer could fire on this
corpus under any implementation. So this ships correct and unit-proven but
INERT on the current cohort, and is reported that way rather than as a measured
win. It also makes INV-karud clause (b) unverifiable on this corpus by
construction, which is evidence Phase 6 needs rather than a gap to paper over.

The coverage gate caught a defensive branch under a green summary (L34, third
time this campaign): "1909 passed" and "(all files at 100%)" printed above
"Coverage failure: total of 99".

